# Memory after code (mandatory)

This applies to **every** coding session in this workspace — Cursor, Claude, Codex, or
subagents — not only when the user types `/maiw`.

## When it applies

Any of:

- You edit, create, or delete files under `apps/<name>/`
- You finish a feature/fix/refactor that changed app behavior
- The user says someone else (e.g. a teammate) pushed app commits and memory may be behind

Skip only for pure Q&A / read-only exploration with **no** durable code or docs change.

## Required finish checklist (do not skip)

Before you treat the task as done:

1. Update `memory/<name>/NOW.md` — current state, what changed, verification, exact next action.
2. Update **only** topic notes your change made stale (paths, behavior, tickets). Do not
   rewrite unrelated topics. Never store secrets.
3. Set `memory/<name>/meta.json` `sourceCommit` to the app HEAD you synchronized (short SHA)
   and refresh `lastSynchronizedAt`.
4. Run from wrapper root: `node bin/memory.js validate <name>` and fix failures.
5. **Auto-commit and auto-push the MAIW wrapper** in the same session (Cursor and Claude
   Code). Do not wait for the user to ask — see `maiw-push-memory`. Verify
   `git log -1 --format=full` is clean of AI trailers before/after.
6. If Jira/Confluence are involved, follow `jira-confluence-source-of-truth` too.

## Incomplete work

Leaving app code updated while `memory/<name>/` is unchanged is a **bug in the session**.
If you cannot update memory (blocked tools, unclear which app), say so explicitly and
tell the user to run `/maiw sync <name>` or ask you to finish memory before closing.

## Teammate / out-of-band commits

If app `main` moved without a memory update (common when someone commits only in
`apps/<name>`), next session must run memory sync for that app before relying on NOW/topics —
prefer `/maiw sync <name>` or an implementer-style memory pass, then push the wrapper.
